0005-device-aware-link-selection.patch
===================================================================
nccl-mesh-plugin @ 19924dcc, on top of 0004-min-rnr-timer.patch.
Files: src/mesh_plugin.c, include/mesh_plugin.h.  No wire-format change:
struct mesh_handle, struct mesh_addr_entry and struct mesh_qp_info are all
untouched, so a patched node and a stock node still hand-shake correctly.

WHAT IT FIXES
-------------
mesh_connect() began with "(void)dev;" and then walked the peer's advertised
address list, stopping at the FIRST address a local NIC could reach.  That is
deterministic, so on a fabric with two cables between every node pair every
NCCL channel to a given peer landed on the same cable and the second cable
carried nothing.  The plugin's own README lists this under "Known limitations"
(no single-peer multi-QP striping or physical-lane aggregation).

WHAT IT DOES
------------
mesh_connect() now enumerates every parallel link to the peer - a candidate is
one (local NIC, peer address) pair sharing a subnet, i.e. one cable - and uses
NCCL's device index to choose among them:

    pick = dev % usable;   usable = min(candidates found, NCCL_MESH_LINKS_PER_PEER)

Candidates are collected in the peer's advertised order and, within one peer
address, in local NIC order.  That is precisely the order the old first-match
search walked, so cands[0] is the NIC it would have returned.  With one cable
per peer - or with NCCL_MESH_LINKS_PER_PEER=1 - the selection is bit-identical
to the pre-patch plugin.

HOW NCCL USES THE MULTIPLE DEVICES  (channels -> devs -> cables)
---------------------------------------------------------------
Nothing changes on NCCL's side; the plumbing was already there and the plugin
was the part throwing the information away.

  * mesh_devices() reports one NCCL net device per physical port (4 on these
    nodes).  It already did.
  * NCCL's graph search builds its rings and assigns each channel a netDev,
    cycling over the devices.  This is visible in NCCL_DEBUG=INFO as
        Channel 00/0 : 2[0] -> 0[0] [receive] via NET/Mesh/0
        Channel 01/0 : 2[0] -> 0[0] [receive] via NET/Mesh/1
    and so on.  One connection - one queue pair - per channel per peer.
  * NCCL passes that netDev to listen()/connect() as `dev`.  connect() now
    honours it.
  * The listener needs no change and gets none: mesh_listen() already opens a
    QP on EVERY NIC and advertises all of them in the handle, and mesh_accept()
    takes its NIC from wherever the handshake arrived - the handshake thread
    picks it from mesh_qp_info.nic_idx, which the connector already fills in
    from the address it selected.  The connector's choice therefore propagates
    to the acceptor over the existing wire format.

Concretely, on a 3-node triangle with two cables per pair, the local NIC order
is nic0 -> peer A, nic1 -> peer B, nic2 -> peer A, nic3 -> peer B.  For traffic
to peer A the candidate list is {nic0, nic2}, and channels with dev 0,1,2,3,...
map to cables 0,1,0,1,... - an even split.  (Honouring `dev` naively, i.e.
"use nics[dev] if it reaches the peer, else fall back to the old search", would
give 6:2 instead of 4:4, because half the devices do not face this peer at all.
That is why the mapping is over the candidate list and not over nics[].)

INTERACTION WITH NCCL_MAX_NCHANNELS=8  -- READ BEFORE MEASURING
---------------------------------------------------------------
NCCL_MAX_NCHANNELS=8 is in production because the plugin's two-sided
SEND/RECV data path has no flow control but the RNR NAK, and with NCCL's
default 64 channels the proxy thread's round-robin lap is long enough that a
sender routinely beats its receiver to the QP (NCCL-MESH-RAPOR.md).  Eight
channels shortens the lap and the RNR counters fall to ~0.

The cap counts CHANNELS, not cables.  With this patch those 8 channels are
split 4 + 4 over the two cables of each peer pair, so each cable now carries
half the queue pairs it did.  Two consequences, both of which the A/B has to
measure rather than assume:

  1. Bandwidth: 4 channels per cable may not be enough to fill a cable at the
     large messages (16-64 MB) where the curve was healthy before.  The likely
     answer is NCCL_MAX_NCHANNELS=16 - 8 per cable, 16 total - which keeps the
     per-cable parallelism the tuned configuration had.  BUT 16 channels was
     measured (NCCL-MESH-RAPOR.md sec 3.1) to bring the RNR misses back at the
     default single-cable arrangement; whether that still holds when the 16 are
     spread over two independent NICs, two CQs and two proxy paths is exactly
     the open question.  Sweep 8 and 16 with the patch, both arms.
  2. RNR: the proxy lap length is set by the total channel count, so at a fixed
     8 channels this patch cannot make RNR worse, and per cable it halves the
     number of QPs competing for the receiver - if anything it should help.

KNOBS
-----
  NCCL_MESH_LINKS_PER_PEER  0 = use every link found (default)
                            1 = pre-patch behaviour, first match only
                            n = cap at n links per peer
  NCCL_MESH_DISTINCT_GUID   0 = report guid 0 for every port (default, and what
                                has been measured to date)
                            1 = report a unique guid per port (the port's
                                fabric IP).  NCCL uses guid to recognise
                                several logical devices that are one physical
                                NIC; reporting 0 for all four says the opposite
                                of the truth.  Untested here, hence opt-in.

TESTS RUN
---------
  make test-unit -> test_routing 13/13.
  test_error_paths (linked by hand; the Makefile's TEST_LDFLAGS still omits
  $(IBVERBS_LIBS)) -> 60 passed / 6 failed, byte-identical to the stock tree.
  Build clean under -Wall -Wextra.
  No fabric test has been run with this patch.
===================================================================

